# ---------- Database ----------
#
# Two connection strings, both required.
#
#   DATABASE_URL  what the app queries through
#   DIRECT_URL    what `prisma db push` changes the schema through
#
# Locally both point at the PostgreSQL service on this machine. There is no
# pooler on a laptop and nothing needs one, but keeping both set means the local
# setup is the same shape as the deployed one.
#
# connection_limit matters. Prisma sizes its pool from the machine's CPU count
# (cores * 2 + 1), and `next build` runs several workers, each with its own
# client — on a 14-core machine that asks for more connections than PostgreSQL's
# default limit of 100 allows, and the build fails partway through with
# "Can't reach database server". Five per client is plenty for this site.
#
# Put your own postgres password in place of PASSWORD. The database itself does
# not need to exist first; `npm run db:push` creates it.

DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/somtel?schema=public&connection_limit=5"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/somtel?schema=public&connection_limit=5"

# ---------- Deployed (Supabase) ----------
#
# A laptop is not reachable from a build server, so a deployment needs a hosted
# database. From the Supabase project: Connect -> ORMs -> Prisma gives both
# strings. They are not interchangeable:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  Transaction pooler, port 6543, plus ?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1
#                 Many short-lived connections, which is what a deployment makes.
#                 Transaction mode has no prepared statements — hence pgbouncer=true,
#                 which tells Prisma to stop using them.
#
#   DIRECT_URL    Direct connection, port 5432, no pooler.
#                 `db push` changes the schema, and a transaction-mode pooler
#                 cannot carry that.
#
# Both go in the host's environment variables. Never commit either one.
#
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# Signs the admin session cookie. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET="replace-me"

# The payment provider behind /api/topup.
#
# Leave TOPUP_API_URL empty and the top-up page still works end to end — it just
# tells the customer payments are not connected yet instead of taking money.
# Both values stay on the server; neither is ever sent to the browser.
#
# The route posts { service, plan, amount, msisdn } as JSON and expects JSON
# back. On success it reads `reference` (or `id`) and `message`; on failure it
# reads `message` or `error` and shows that to the customer.
TOPUP_API_URL=""
TOPUP_API_KEY=""
